<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="methylglyoxal degradation VIII" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: METHYL-GLYOXAL &quot;Methylglyoxal&quot;| is produced in small amounts during glycolysis (via |FRAME:DIHYDROXY-ACETONE-PHOSPHATE|), fatty acid metabolism (via |FRAME:ACETONE|), and protein metabolism (via |FRAME:AMINO-ACETONE|). |FRAME: METHYL-GLYOXAL &quot;Methylglyoxal&quot;| is highly toxic, most likely as a result of its interaction with protein side chains (see |CITS: [10597025]| for a review). There are several pathways for the detoxification of methylglyoxal, based on different enzymes that are able to convert methylglyoxal to less toxic compounds. These enzymes include glyoxalase enzymes, methylglyoxal reductases, aldose reductases, aldehyde reductases and methylglyoxal dehydrogenases. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; This pathway for methylglyoxal degradation has been characterized from the yeast |FRAME: TAX-4932|. This pathway is based on a glyoxalase system in which |FRAME: METHYL-GLYOXAL| is first condensed with |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| to give |FRAME: S-LACTOYL-GLUTATHIONE| by the action of glyoxalase I (|FRAME: YML004C|) |CITS: [8824231]|, followed by hydrolysis of the glutathione thiolester to |FRAME: D-LACTATE| and |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| by glyoxalase II (|FRAME: YDR272W| and |FRAME: YOR040W|) |CITS: [9261170]|. The |FRAME: YDR272W| gene is expressed when the yeast cells are grown on either glucose or glycerol and the active Glo2p protein is localized in the cytoplasm. The |FRAME: YOR040W| gene is only active during growth on glycerol and the active Glo4p is localized in the mitochondrial matrix |CITS: [9261170]|. Deletion of either |FRAME: YML004C| or |FRAME: YDR272W| and |FRAME: YOR040W| results in increased sensitivity to exogenous methylglyoxal |CITS: [8824231]||CITS: [9261170]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
